Thought: My config improved to 0.6847 (higher LR 1e-3.5, lower dropout 0.15, bigger batch 64, shape 0.7 all helped vs baseline). Agent2's small/low-LR config was bad (0.554). Direction: higher LR and lower dropout seem good. Agent1 is testing lr=1e-3 with 4 layers; agent3 tests extreme. Let me push further along my improving direction but distinct: try even higher LR (1e-3 is being tested by agent1, so try -3.2-ish... actually agent1 uses -3). Let me try initial_lr_log10=-3.0? That overlaps with agent1's LR but different other params. Better: keep exploring around my best — try num_layers=4, units=7, dropout=0.1, lr=-3.5, shape=0.8. But agent1 covers units=7/layers=4. Instead try units=6, layers=3, lr=-3.0, dropout=0.1, batch=6, shape=0.6 to isolate LR effect.

Given time (2149s left, ~96s per eval → ~20 evals), I can afford several. Next: test higher LR with my recipe.
Action: evaluate_config {"average_units_per_layer_log2":6.5,"batch_size_log2":6.0,"dropout_0":0.1,"dropout_1":0.1,"final_lr_fraction_log2":-2.5,"initial_lr_log10":-3.0,"num_layers":3,"shape_parameter_1":0.7}

Thought: Higher LR helped: lr=-3.0 with dropout 0.1 → 0.7092. Agent1's config (units=7, layers=4, lr=-3, dropout=0.2) got 0.7131, slightly better. Trends: lr around 1e-3 is good; lower dropout helps; more capacity (units=7, layers=4) helps. Agent1 is now exploring units=7.5/layers=4/dropout=0.3. Agent3 doing extreme 8/5L.

I should combine best signals but avoid duplicating agent1's in-progress config. Try: units=7, layers=4, dropout=0.1, lr=-3.0, batch=6, shape=0.7, final_lr_fraction=-2.5. This merges agent1's capacity with my better dropout/shape.

Action: evaluate_config {"average_units_per_layer_log2":7.0,"batch_size_log2":6.0,"dropout_0":0.1,"dropout_1":0.1,"final_lr_fraction_log2":-2.5,"initial_lr_log10":-3.0,"num_layers":4,"shape_parameter_1":0.7}

Thought: Best is agent1's 0.716121: units=7.5, batch=6, dropout=0.3/0.3, final_lr=-2, lr=-3, layers=4, shape=0.5. Interesting — higher dropout 0.3 with wider 7.5 layers helped. Shape 0.6 didn't help (0.7129 vs 0.7131). Trends: width 7.5 good, dropout 0.3 good at larger width, lr ~-3 to -2.5 good, shape 0.5 fine.

Agent1 in progress: 7.5/6/0.25/lr-2.8/4L/0.5. Agent3: 7.5/6/0.3,0.1/lr-2.2/4L/0.5. I should explore an uncovered tweak of the best: units=7.5, batch=6, dropout=0.35, final_lr=-2, lr=-3.0, layers=4, shape=0.5 — testing even higher dropout at this width. Or try units=8 with dropout 0.3, layers=4 (agent3's 8/5L was bad partly due to 5 layers and extreme params). Let me try units=8, layers=4, dropout=0.3, batch=6, lr=-3, final_lr=-2, shape=0.5.

Action: evaluate_config {"average_units_per_layer_log2":8.0,"batch_size_log2":6.0,"dropout_0":0.3,"dropout_1":0.3,"final_lr_fraction_log2":-2.0,"initial_lr_log10":-3.0,"num_layers":4,"shape_parameter_1":0.5}

Thought: Best is 0.716734 (agent1): units=7.5, batch=6, dropout=0.25/0.25, final_lr=-2, lr=-2.8, layers=4, shape=0.5. My 8.0 config got 0.716158, close. The surface is quite flat near the optimum (~0.716). Time left: 1302s; evals are getting expensive (398s for the 8.0 config). Wider configs cost more. I should stick with 7.5-width region and try small perturbations not covered by agents 1 and 3 (they're testing shape=0.6 and final_lr=-3.5 variants of the best).

An untested tweak: dropout=0.25 with lr=-2.6, or batch=6.5, or shape=0.4. Let me try shape=0.4 with best config (shape hasn't been explored below 0.5 except agent3's 0.3 at different config). Actually agent1 is doing shape=0.6. Try shape=0.4: units=7.5, batch=6, dropout=0.25, final_lr=-2, lr=-2.8, layers=4, shape=0.4.

